1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,332
He turned $5,000 into $200,000. Then he studied why.

2
00:00:05,333 --> 00:00:08,405
In 1958 he became a stockbroker.

3
00:00:08,406 --> 00:00:13,332
In early 1962 his rules said sell everything. Then stocks crashed.

4
00:00:13,333 --> 00:00:16,776
Three big trades turned $5,000 into $200,000.

5
00:00:16,777 --> 00:00:21,332
At 30 he bought a New York Stock Exchange seat.

6
00:00:21,333 --> 00:00:26,138
Why a legend? He studied winners by computer in the 1960s.

7
00:00:26,139 --> 00:00:30,583
He measured over 1,000 big winners, back to the 1880s.

8
00:00:30,584 --> 00:00:34,666
Then he shared it all in a daily paper.

9
00:00:34,667 --> 00:00:39,880
Before big runs, winners often rested in a base: a sideways pause.

10
00:00:39,881 --> 00:00:43,269
His favorite was the cup with handle.

11
00:00:43,270 --> 00:00:48,118
That's a rounded dip, then a smaller dip near the top.

12
00:00:48,119 --> 00:00:53,332
He bought when the price broke above the handle, on heavy volume.

13
00:00:53,333 --> 00:00:58,666
His sell rule: cut every loss 7 to 8% below your buy.

14
00:00:58,667 --> 00:01:03,999
A small loss is easy to win back. A big one isn't.

15
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,529
Even legends slip. His fund rose 116% in 1967.

16
00:01:08,530 --> 00:01:14,264
Then stocks fell, investors pulled out, and assets shrank to $6 million.

17
00:01:14,265 --> 00:01:19,999
His take: they grew too fast and bought too many small stocks.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,326
His rules fit on a card. Buy leaders breaking out.

19
00:01:24,327 --> 00:01:29,005
Cut losses at 7 to 8%. Don't own too many stocks.

20
00:01:29,006 --> 00:01:33,332
And follow the market. Most stocks go where it goes.

21
00:01:33,333 --> 00:01:38,666
Legend card 02. Study his winners, and his rules.

22
00:01:41,533 --> 00:01:46,616
Lore 010. Next: Lore 011, The RS line leads price
